00:27:37,856 --> 00:27:44,496
Let's proceed with now how we will solve the other four problems we discussed above.

204
00:27:44,576 --> 00:27:49,764
So we have now solved the NAT and firewall problem, but we still had four other problems, right?

205
00:27:50,542 --> 00:28:00,484
We had problem where multiple client want to connect to same AI agent, or we had problems about how agent will handle multiple simultaneous peer-to-peer connections.

206
00:28:00,816 --> 00:28:05,250
What if there is a lot of peer-to-peer connections between client1, client two, one agent two?

207
00:28:05,456 --> 00:28:07,044
There is a mess of connections.

208
00:28:07,278 --> 00:28:10,244
How to manage all those things, like I have shown you here.

209
00:28:10,606 --> 00:28:28,932
And as I have said earlier, if user one needs to share a video, let's say they are in a meeting room and user one needs to share his video using his camera, that means user one's client need to share the same video feed to both user two and user three, right?

210
00:28:29,136 --> 00:28:33,696
And that will actually eat up the bandwidth of user one's device unnecessarily.

211
00:28:33,902 --> 00:28:34,948
Why would you have to do that?

212
00:28:35,822 --> 00:28:37,860
But that's a problem with a peer-to-peer mess.

213
00:28:42,318 --> 00:28:44,292
How does LiveKit solve this problem?

214
00:28:44,496 --> 00:28:49,252
Is something called SFU or Selective Forwarding Unit.

215
00:28:49,376 --> 00:28:50,976
Now there is a really good article.

216
00:28:51,136 --> 00:28:52,676
I have linked down here.

217
00:28:53,056 --> 00:29:02,336
Go through this article, which will give you if you want to know more about SFU or the different other options that is available to solve this problem.

218
00:29:02,638 --> 00:29:04,096
This article talks about it.

219
00:29:04,558 --> 00:29:06,276
Actually, I'll just quickly open it for you.

220
00:29:06,766 --> 00:29:14,816
So here it says that when two peers connect one another, they exchange ignoring data channels, audio and video directly.

221
00:29:14,896 --> 00:29:17,124
This works well for set of two to three peers.

222
00:29:17,856 --> 00:29:25,508
But how many people have internet connection which can consistently upload 5, 7, 20 pixel video stream simultaneously?

223
00:29:26,016 --> 00:29:26,244
Right?

224
00:29:26,862 --> 00:29:27,376
Not many.

225
00:29:27,598 --> 00:29:33,700
Thus, scaling Weber TC to group of more than two to three people require a client server model.

226
00:29:34,062 --> 00:29:43,044
Again, that means you will say, Hey Avijet, you just said that the client server architecture doesn't work, and now you are talking about you will go back to the client server architecture.

227
00:29:43,950 --> 00:29:52,772
Yes and no, you will see how these things are being resolved using the client server architecture on top of WebRTC using SFU.

228
00:29:53,056 --> 00:30:01,936
So basically, without an SFU, what is happening is you have your AI agent have multiple connections with different users.

229
00:30:02,126 --> 00:30:03,844
Let's say if they're on the same meeting room.

230
00:30:05,038 --> 00:30:07,012
So that means you have n connection required.

231
00:30:07,136 --> 00:30:24,996
But what LiveKit is saying is you have one SFU, which is the selective forward thing unit, and both the clients which are user 1, user 2, user 3, and many more clients there could be, they all will be connected to SFU, and the AI agent will also connect it to SFU.

232
00:30:25,390 --> 00:30:31,216
And then SFU will manage the transfer of data like the video tracks, text data, and everything.

233
00:30:31,662 --> 00:30:38,460
It SFU will manage it using the best latency free and bandwidth compatible way.

234
00:30:39,342 --> 00:30:41,792
You don't have to worry about it how it is doing it.

235
00:30:41,952 --> 00:30:50,756
You just write your AI agent code, or you just write your WebRTC client, and all you need to do is just point to the SFU, and the SFU will take care of it.

236
00:30:51,278 --> 00:30:54,112
There is a classic problem that I tried to explain it here.

237
00:30:54,272 --> 00:31:04,740
That means let's say there are five clients or like four client and one agent, or similar like that, they all went into a meeting room which is powered by WebRTC.

238
00:31:05,358 --> 00:31:13,316
Now let's say three of the client have shared their audio and video both, and two of the client have only shared their audio.

239
00:31:13,632 --> 00:31:20,000
But these two client who have only shared their audio, they want to consume the video from all these three client.

240
00:31:20,622 --> 00:31:26,692
But this three client let's say two client only want to consume the audio from the other participants, not the video.

241
00:31:27,182 --> 00:31:31,300
And let's say this participant want to consume both audio and video.

242
00:31:31,918 --> 00:31:36,832
Now this is a really complex setup when it comes to a peer-to-peer setup, right?

243
00:31:36,912 --> 00:31:46,660
Because in a peer-to-peer setup, it's very difficult for to manage such a system because let's say this client want audio video from everyone.

244
00:31:47,342 --> 00:31:54,884
so that means this client has to share the video to this user in a peer-to-peer network.

245
00:31:55,310 --> 00:31:57,316
and this user using three channels.

246
00:31:57,952 --> 00:32:05,632
Whereas this client may be just interested in audio, so all this client have to produce the audio for each of the clients, right?

247
00:32:05,712 --> 00:32:07,552
So this makes it really really complicated.

248
00:32:07,712 --> 00:32:12,832
In case of SFU, what happens is everyone goes to LivePed SFU and SFU manage it.

249
00:32:12,912 --> 00:32:17,472
So basically, this client have to subscribe to the tracks that they need to access.

250
00:32:17,632 --> 00:32:20,432
For example, an AI agent is a voiceai agent.

251
00:32:20,512 --> 00:32:26,752
So if it is a voice AI agent, there is no need for the voice AI agent to subscribe to a video track, right?

252
00:32:26,832 --> 00:32:33,072
So you when you design your AI agent system, you would say, I don't want a video track.

253
00:32:33,232 --> 00:32:44,484
All I would do is just subscribe to audio tracks, or maybe just subscribe to the data stream where you get the text output because your AI agent maybe is meant for only chat, it doesn't have multimodal use case.

254
00:32:44,592 --> 00:32:56,644
So based on that, you define your AI agent, it will go to the SFU and it will only subscribe for let's say audio track or data or data stream, and then SFU will actually manage the track distribution.

255
00:32:57,262 --> 00:32:58,272
Does it make sense to you?

256
00:32:58,574 --> 00:33:02,992
So that's how they basically live kit is managing the SFU.

257
00:33:03,072 --> 00:33:10,340
So LiveKit node have SFU nodes built in it, and it also have the turn server and stun server built in it.

258
00:33:11,566 --> 00:33:22,148
So when you are going to self-host a live kit server in a node, that node will have SFU and it will have stun and turn servers built into it.

259
00:33:23,712 --> 00:33:24,032
But

260
00:33:28,526 --> 00:33:39,952
the question is if there is one SFU managing media streams from 5, 10, 15 clients, this SFU must be exhausted, right?

261
00:33:40,112 --> 00:33:53,092
If there are too many media streams that it needs to process, so SFU actually that is where is better than MCU, which they have discussed in this blog.

262
00:33:53,582 --> 00:34:03,492
That SFU is horizontally scalable, that means you can have multiple nodes, SFU nodes you can spin up as and when you need more and more processing.

263
00:34:03,872 --> 00:34:12,516
But then the question is how the SFU node would ensure that same track is not being reprocessed by different node.

264
00:34:12,752 --> 00:34:13,552
You don't want that, right?

265
00:34:13,632 --> 00:34:19,748
Let's say client to have sent their video stream and that is already processed and sent to other clients.

266
00:34:20,174 --> 00:34:24,964
You don't want the new node, which is node 2, to reprocess that again.

267
00:34:25,742 --> 00:34:31,940
For that, there is a Redis cluster that you need to spin up if you want to sell FOST Live kit.

268
00:34:32,352 --> 00:34:41,348
And this Redis is where the node will connect to and it will save the session state, it will save the context, it will save the media states and everything.

269
00:34:41,774 --> 00:34:52,484
So that new nodes as they spin up or as they are operational in a cluster, they basically share they basically get the state of the information and they basically process it properly.

270
00:34:53,102 --> 00:34:59,392
So that means in a true live kit architecture, you need to have a load balancer.

271
00:34:59,472 --> 00:35:11,972
That means each client or agent needs to come through the load balancer and connect to the nodes for the SFU services, and these nodes will be connected to the Redis.

272
00:35:12,398 --> 00:35:22,244
Even the better architecture will be to have even a Redis cluster available so that in case of any fallback that is needed in a production setup, you have a Redis replica as well.

273
00:35:23,278 --> 00:35:35,588
And trust me, this is one of the reasons why, even in my SaaS project, even though I'm using LiveKit, I am taking my hands off from hosting LiveKit myself.

