00:38:13,296 --> 00:38:20,996
Now, in a WebRTC framework, you do not want to use RTMP streaming because that is overkill for WebRTC framework.

297
00:38:21,870 --> 00:38:26,244
You really need some lightweight way to share the media streams.

298
00:38:27,694 --> 00:38:38,212
Now, if any of these text-to-speech services generate the media in a format which WebRTC doesn't support out of the box, then there is a problem, right?

299
00:38:38,496 --> 00:38:49,796
Because you have your UI running, you have your clients running, and those clients won't be able to ingest those media streams that your AI agent is producing.

300
00:38:51,118 --> 00:39:04,804
So that is why there is a protocol that is supported in WebRTC HTTP ingestion protocol, and live kit actually internally manages a web server as well.

301
00:39:05,134 --> 00:39:21,860
So that means, whenever your AI agent initialize any stream, basically, then this WHIP server basically check if that stream needs to be transcoded for all of the end users or web or web.c client.

302
00:39:22,336 --> 00:39:24,836
and if that is supported by this webrc client.

303
00:39:25,136 --> 00:39:40,420
If if needed, then the web server will basically transcode it and then it will forward it to the end user through SFU, or if there is no need, then it will basically directly transfer the communications or the streams to the end user.

304
00:39:41,198 --> 00:39:58,340
Because it is using WebRTC HTTP ingestion protocol, that is the reason why this WebRTC powered AI agents are so fast because this protocol ensures that the media streamings that is happening through WebRTC are low latency distribution.

